Single-database configuration for Flask.

API overview

This backend is a JWT-authenticated Flask API for managing a user’s personal Maryland hiking to‑hike list (“trails”).


Tech stack: Flask, SQLAlchemy, Alembic/Flask‑Migrate, JWT (HS256), SQLite (instance/dev.db)
Core models:
User – id, email (unique, case‑insensitive), password_hash, role (user/admin), created_at
Trail – id, owner_id → User.id, name, location, distance_miles, difficulty, notes, priority, created_at
Authentication & authorization

Register: POST /auth/register

Body: email (valid, max 255 chars), password (8–128 chars)
On success: 201 with id, email, created_at
Uses user.set_password() to store only a hash.
Login: POST /auth/login

Body: email, password
On success: returns a JWT access token (HS256) with sub (user id), role, exp.
Auth rules:

Missing, expired, or invalid token → 401 with {"error": "unauthorized", "message": ...}
All /trails routes require a valid token.
Trail access is owner‑scoped: each user only sees and mutates their own trails.
Other users’ trails behave as if they don’t exist → 404 {"error": "not_found", "message": "trail not found"}.
Admin route:

GET /admin/ping
Normal users → 403 {"error": "forbidden", "message": "admin access required"}
Admins → 200 {"admin": "<email>", "status": "ok"}
User endpoints

GET /auth/me – Return the current user profile (id, email, created_at, maybe role if you include it).
PATCH /auth/me – Update the current user (e.g. email/password as allowed by your implementation).
Trail endpoints (owner-scoped)

All of these require Authorization: Bearer <token> and operate only on the current user’s trails.


POST /trails

Create a new trail owned by the current user.
Returns 201 with full trail JSON (including id, owner_id/implied owner via token, created_at).
GET /trails (paginated list)

Query params: page (default 1), per_page (default 10).

Returns 200 with:

{
  "items": [ /* this user's trails only, up to per_page */ ],
  "page": 1,
  "pages": 3,
  "per_page": 10,
  "total": 25
}
GET /trails/<id>

Returns the trail if it belongs to the current user → 200 with trail JSON.
For non‑existent or other users’ trails → 404 {"error": "not_found", "message": "trail not found"}.
For non‑numeric IDs (e.g. /trails/abc) → global JSON 404: {"error": "not_found", "message": "resource not found"}.
PATCH /trails/<id>

Partially update an owned trail.
Returns 200 with updated trail JSON, or 404 as above.
DELETE /trails/<id>

Delete an owned trail.
Returns 204 NO CONTENT on success, or 404 as above.